WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.796 --> 00:00:07.757
We were expecting a river here and we didn’t find one.

00:00:14.097 --> 00:00:18.393
In 2015, a group of scientists began a comprehensive survey 

00:00:18.393 --> 00:00:21.646
of the little known Angolan highlands.

00:00:21.896 --> 00:00:25.859
The plan was to travel thousands of kilometers down river 

00:00:25.859 --> 00:00:29.362
from the source lakes to Botswana’s Okavango Delta

00:00:29.362 --> 00:00:32.032
to learn more about this critical ecosystem.

00:00:33.116 --> 00:00:35.994
Just days after launching canoes from the lake, 

00:00:35.994 --> 00:00:38.955
the team found only a small stream – 

00:00:38.955 --> 00:00:42.167
not enough to float their 400 kilogram boats. 

00:00:43.585 --> 00:00:45.754
But there are no other options. There is no vehicle. Drop-off point. 

00:00:45.754 --> 00:00:48.506
There is no other way for us to get to the water we can use.

00:00:58.767 --> 00:01:01.728
But an almost expedition-ending problem

00:01:01.728 --> 00:01:05.273
became an important scientific discovery.

00:01:05.523 --> 00:01:08.902
The soggy terrain the team was dragging its boats across

00:01:08.902 --> 00:01:11.946
wasn’t blocking the river from its source,

00:01:11.946 --> 00:01:14.741
it was bridging it.

00:01:16.701 --> 00:01:20.080
They were trekking across peat – 

00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:24.667
a rich soil made up of partially decaying vegetation,

00:01:24.667 --> 00:01:28.713
able to hold ten times its weight in water.

00:01:29.964 --> 00:01:33.343
Like a 1,600-square-kilometer-sponge,

00:01:33.343 --> 00:01:35.428
these peat deposits are filtering

00:01:35.428 --> 00:01:38.848
and feeding pristine water into the Okavango. 

00:01:39.057 --> 00:01:42.644
This steady release ensures that even in drier years, 

00:01:42.644 --> 00:01:45.396
the water keeps flowing. 

00:01:45.396 --> 00:01:47.398
One, two, three. 

00:01:47.398 --> 00:01:50.318
On the water. Yay!

00:01:52.695 --> 00:01:53.905
Since then,

00:01:56.157 --> 00:02:00.286
the team has conducted more in-depth studies of the peat. 

00:02:01.287 --> 00:02:02.872
C'mon. C'mon.

00:02:03.706 --> 00:02:04.749
Yes!

00:02:06.292 --> 00:02:08.545
Years of collecting samples

00:02:08.545 --> 00:02:10.296
and radiocarbon dating

00:02:10.296 --> 00:02:12.715
have revealed that for thousands of years

00:02:12.715 --> 00:02:17.679
 this living landscape has been absorbing carbon dioxide from the atmosphere,

00:02:17.679 --> 00:02:20.974
helping to mitigate the global effects of climate change.

00:02:22.809 --> 00:02:27.438
Thousands of tons of carbon are being sequestered by the Angolan highlands each year.

00:02:27.605 --> 00:02:31.359
But they’ve also discovered that these critical peat ecosystems

00:02:31.359 --> 00:02:33.653
are threatened by human impacts

00:02:33.653 --> 00:02:35.113
like fires  

00:02:35.113 --> 00:02:37.115
and encroaching agriculture. 

00:02:37.907 --> 00:02:40.952
We’ve only begun to understand these peatlands. 

00:02:40.952 --> 00:02:45.165
National Geographic and De Beers’ Okavango Eternal partnership

00:02:45.165 --> 00:02:49.961
is supporting PhD researchers to study and map the area. 

00:02:50.295 --> 00:02:54.632
Providing evidence about why and how to protect these peatlands; 

00:02:54.632 --> 00:02:56.801
the biodiversity they support,

00:02:56.801 --> 00:02:59.596
and the water and carbon they regulate.

00:03:00.013 --> 00:03:05.894
Okavango Eternal will use these findings to inform the creation of conserved areas, 

00:03:05.894 --> 00:03:08.688
which are supported by local communities.

00:03:09.355 --> 00:03:11.816
Not only helping to protect the peatlands

00:03:11.816 --> 00:03:13.985
and the rest of the Okavango Basin,

00:03:14.402 --> 00:03:17.906
but also creating sustainable livelihood opportunities

00:03:17.906 --> 00:03:20.408
for the people who rely on it.

